; Startup-Sequence: the boot shell reads this once, before its first
; prompt - the shell dos's init starts in a window. Every shell after it,
; `s3> newshell`'s included, reads S:Shell-Startup instead.

; Answers with the system's version and leaves it in the local variable
; Kickstart, which the SetEnv below puts where anything can read it.
C:Version >NIL:

FailAt 21

C:MakeDir >NIL: RAM:T RAM:Clipboards RAM:ENV RAM:ENV/Sys

; Kept in memory: loaded once and run from there until Resident takes it
; off the list again. Assign is cached because the lines below call it.
Resident >NIL: C:Assign PURE
Resident >NIL: C:Dir PURE
Resident >NIL: C:List PURE

; LIBS: is two directories: the libraries, and the classes a program opens
; as gadgets/<name>.gadget, which live in SYS:classes.
Assign >NIL: LIBS: SYS:libs SYS:classes

; FONTS: - each family a contents file and a directory of its sizes.
Assign >NIL: FONTS: SYS:fonts

; The global variables that survive a reboot live on the disk; the ones a
; running system reads live in RAM:, and start as a copy of them.
Assign >NIL: ENVARC: SYS:Prefs/Env-Archive
C:Copy >NIL: ENVARC: RAM:ENV ALL QUIET NOREQ

Assign >NIL: ENV: RAM:ENV
Assign >NIL: T: RAM:T
Assign >NIL: CLIPS: RAM:Clipboards

; The kinds of file the system knows, from DEVS:DataTypes into the list
; datatypes.library works from. Without it nothing opens a file by what
; is in it.
C:AddDataTypes >NIL: QUIET

; The system's fonts, as ENV:Sys/font.prefs names them: every screen,
; window and console opened from here on uses them.
C:FontPrefs >NIL:

; The system's style, as ENV:Sys/style.prefs gives it: every screen takes
; it, those already open too.
C:StylePrefs >NIL:

; intuition's own settings, as ENV:Sys/intuition.prefs gives them: the
; double-click time, the screens' font height, the keyboard on the screen.
C:IPrefs >NIL:

; Where a command is looked for, before C:. C:test holds the programs that
; exercise a device or a library, C:net the network's tools, so they are
; called by name like the rest.
Path >NIL: RAM: SYS: S: C:test C:net

; The network, from DEVS:NetInterfaces/, and then the clock from a time
; server: in a shell of its own, so the boot waits for neither. A board
; without an interface's device says nothing and loses no time.
Run >NIL: Execute S:Network-Startup

SetEnv Kickstart $Kickstart
UnSet Kickstart

If EXISTS S:User-Startup
  Execute S:User-Startup
EndIf
